  "id": "Tropospheric Column NO₂ by Sentinel-5P TROPOMI (Daily)",
  "stac_version": "1.1.0",
  "description": "## Tropospheric Column NO₂ by Sentinel-5P TROPOMI (Daily)\nNitrogen dioxide (NO₂) is a short-lived air pollutant produced primarily by human activity, combustion of fossil fuels from road traffic, shipping, power plants, and industrial facilities. Burning activities and wildfires also contribute significantly. NO₂ has a direct impact on human health and indirectly contributes to the formation of ozone and fine particles. Because NO₂ concentrations respond rapidly to changes in emissions, they serve as useful indicators of economic activity and the effectiveness of emission reduction policies.\n\n## About the dataset\n- The Tropospheric Column NO₂product is measured  by the [TROPOMI instrument](https://www.tropomi.eu/) aboard ESA’s [Sentinel-5P Precursor](https://www.esa.int/Applications/Observing_the_Earth/Copernicus/Sentinel-5P) satellite.\n- The product provides the tropospheric NO₂ column between the surface and the top of the troposphere, as well as stratospheric and total column data.\n- Because TROPOMI cannot see through thick clouds, near-surface concentrations can only be retrieved under cloud-free or partly cloudy conditions.\n- More information on the TROPOMI NO₂ measurements and quality assessment can be found in the [Product Readme file](https://sentiwiki.copernicus.eu/__attachments/1673595/S5P-MPC-KNMI-PRF-NO2%20-%20Sentinel-5P%20Nitrogen%20Dioxide%20Level%202%20Product%20Readme%20File%202025%20-%202.8.pdf).\n\n## Use cases\nNO₂ measurements are particularly useful for:\n- Monitoring air quality and anthropogenic pollution from traffic, industry, and power generation at urban and regional scales.\n- Tracking emission reductions from policy interventions, economic slowdowns, or lockdown measures.\n- Detecting NO₂ from wildfires, biomass burning, and shipping lanes.\n",
